### 4. What is Regeneron's monoclonal antibody cocktail (REGN-COV2) composed of, and how was it developed?
Answer: REGN-COV2 is "a cocktail of two fully human antibodies, REGN10933 and REGN10987, that bind to spike protein, potently neutralize SARS-CoV-2 and were selected as components of anti-viral antibody cocktail (REGN-COV2) to safeguard against mutational virus escape." The two antibodies target "non-overlapping epitopes on the SARS-CoV-2 spike protein." According to the referenced works, the antibodies were identified through studies in "humanized mice and convalescent humans" (Hansen et al., 2020), and were selected as a cocktail specifically because using two antibodies targeting different sites on the spike protein reduces the likelihood of viral escape mutants that readily emerge with single-antibody treatment. Notably, this source does not mention the use of fetal tissue in the development process.

Source URL: https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2020.08.02.233320v1.full.pdf

### 5. Were human embryonic fetal tissue or fetal cell lines used in the development or creation of Regeneron's REGN-COV2 antibody treatment?
Answer: Yes, human embryonic fetal cell lines were used in the development and testing of Regeneron's REGN-COV2 antibody treatment. According to the article, while the antibodies are manufactured in hamster ovary cells, laboratory tests to assess their potency used a standardized supply of HEK 293T cells, which "originated from kidney tissue from an abortion in the Netherlands in the 1970s."

Source URL: https://www.technologyreview.com/2020/10/07/1009664/trumps-antibody-treatment-was-tested-using-cells-from-an-abortion/

